<Base
  title="Phase-sensing interferometry for SiV nanophotonic cavities"
  description="A fiber Mach–Zehnder interferometer, its lock–probe measurement protocol, and the desktop application that automate coupling-regime characterization of silicon-vacancy nanophotonic cavities."
>
  <article class="shell">
    <p class="back"><a href={base + '/research'}>← Research</a></p>

    <header class="proj-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">2026 · Lukin Group, Department of Physics, Harvard University</p>
      <h1>A Phase-Sensing Mach–Zehnder Interferometer for Coupling-Regime Characterization of SiV Nanophotonic Cavities</h1>
      <p class="proj-blurb">
        The reflected <em>intensity</em> of a single-sided optical cavity cannot distinguish an
        over-coupled device from an under-coupled one — near critical coupling both produce the
        same dip. The reflected <em>phase</em> can. This is the fiber interferometer built to
        measure it, together with the electronics, the interleaved lock–probe protocol, and the
        automated pipeline that turn interference fringes into cavity parameters.
      </p>

      <dl class="proj-meta">
        <div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Visiting Undergraduate Research Fellow · with Siyuan Xu</dd></div>
        <div>
          <dt>Topics</dt>
          <dd class="tags">
            <span class="tag">fiber interferometry</span>
            <span class="tag">cavity QED</span>
            <span class="tag">AOM phase control</span>
            <span class="tag">servo design</span>
            <span class="tag">instrument automation</span>
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Materials</dt>
          <dd class="links">
            <a href={base + '/pdfs/research/phase_interferometry_report.pdf'} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Full project report (PDF, 35 pp) ↗</a>
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Why intensity is not enough</h2>
      <p>
        Diamond nanophotonic cavities containing silicon-vacancy centers are the light–matter
        interface of our quantum-network experiments. Each device is characterised by a resonance
        frequency <em>f</em><sub>res</sub> and two loss channels: the external coupling rate
        <em>κ</em><sub>ex</sub> into the access waveguide, and the intrinsic loss <em>κ</em><sub>i</sub>
        — scattering, absorption, leakage into other modes. Whether a fabricated device is worth
        using as a network node comes down to which of the two dominates: an over-coupled cavity
        returns most of its intracavity light to the collection channel, an under-coupled one loses
        it internally.
      </p>
      <p>
        For a single-sided cavity probed in reflection, input–output theory gives the amplitude
        reflection coefficient at detuning Δ = <em>f</em> − <em>f</em><sub>res</sub>:
      </p>

      <div class="eq">
        <em>r</em>(Δ) = 1 −
        <span class="frac"><span class="num"><em>κ</em><sub>ex</sub></span><span class="den"><em>κ</em>/2 + <em>i</em>Δ</span></span>
        =
        <span class="frac"><span class="num">(<em>κ</em><sub>i</sub> − <em>κ</em><sub>ex</sub>)/2 + <em>i</em>Δ</span><span class="den">(<em>κ</em><sub>i</sub> + <em>κ</em><sub>ex</sub>)/2 + <em>i</em>Δ</span></span>
      </div>

      <p>
        On resonance this collapses to |<em>r</em>(0)| = |<em>κ</em><sub>i</sub> − <em>κ</em><sub>ex</sub>|/<em>κ</em>,
        which is <strong>invariant under exchanging <em>κ</em><sub>i</sub> and <em>κ</em><sub>ex</sub></strong>.
        That single symmetry is the whole problem: a reflection-intensity spectrum cannot tell the
        two regimes apart, and near critical coupling both look like the same near-total dip.
      </p>
      <p>
        The phase breaks the degeneracy. Sweeping across resonance, an over-coupled cavity has a
        numerator that encircles the origin and its phase winds through a full 360°; a critically
        coupled one passes through zero and jumps by 180°; an under-coupled one makes a bounded
        excursion of less than 180° and returns to baseline. Since photodetectors measure only
        intensity, reading that phase requires an interferometer.
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={couplingRegimes} alt="Calculated reflection of a single-sided cavity for under-coupled, critically coupled and over-coupled devices; top row reflection phase, bottom row magnitude, with the magnitude dips of the outer columns identical." widths={[600, 1000, 1400]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          Calculated reflection at fixed total linewidth <em>κ</em> = 100 GHz. The magnitude dips of
          the two outer columns are <em>identical</em>; the phase either returns to baseline
          (under-coupled) or winds through a full turn (over-coupled).
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <CriticalityDivider label="the instrument" />

      <h2>Reading the phase out</h2>
      <p>
        The cavity sits in one arm of a fiber Mach–Zehnder interferometer. The two complementary
        outputs carry the interference term, and the total interferometer phase separates into four
        physically distinct contributions:
      </p>

      <div class="eq">
        Δ<em>φ</em>(<em>f</em>) = 2π<em>f</em>Δ<em>τ</em> + <em>φ</em><sub>c</sub>(<em>f</em>) + <em>K v</em><sub>mod</sub> + <em>φ</em><sub>0</sub>(<em>t</em>)
        <span class="eq-where">arm imbalance · cavity · AOM control · slow drift</span>
      </div>

      <p>
        Only the second term is wanted, and the measurement design is essentially a strategy for
        removing the other three. <em>K v</em><sub>mod</sub> is what we deliberately sweep and
        servo; <em>φ</em><sub>0</sub>(<em>t</em>) is cancelled by interleaving a phase-locked
        reference laser; and the linear background 2π<em>f</em>Δ<em>τ</em> is measured and
        subtracted through the interferometer's free spectral range.
      </p>
      <p>
        The interferometer is fiber throughout, with two deliberate free-space excursions. A fixed
        Matisse ring laser at 406.545 06 THz serves as the lock reference and a tunable Toptica DL pro
        as the probe; each is gated by its own preparation AOM, so TTL lines select which laser is in
        the interferometer at any instant. The delay arm carries roughly 8 m of PM fiber and a
        retroreflector on a 225 mm motorized stage — double-passed, so 1 mm of carriage travel moves
        the optical path by 2 mm. The free arm passes through a circulator to a tapered fiber that
        couples evanescently to the cavity. Each arm's AOM runs continuously at 200 MHz and acts as
        the fast phase actuator.
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={opticalLayout} alt="Optical layout of the phase-sensing Mach-Zehnder interferometer showing the two lasers, preparation AOMs, input beamsplitter, delay arm with retroreflector stage, free arm with circulator and cavity, recombining beamsplitter and two photodiodes." widths={[600, 1000, 1240]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          Optical layout. The lock and probe lasers are gated by their preparation AOMs and combined
          at the input beamsplitter, which also splits the light into the delay arm (retroreflector
          stage, right) and the free arm (cavity behind the circulator, left). Both recombine on the
          output coupler, read by photodiodes PD1 and PD2. A Red Pitaya digitises both and drives the
          delay-arm AOM's phase modulation through an op-amp stage.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={setupPhotos} alt="Four photographs of the setup: the main interferometer board, the laser preparation breadboard, the delay-stage assembly with retroreflector rail and fiber spools, and the microscope station above the diamond sample." widths={[600, 1000, 1500]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          The bench. Clockwise from top left: the main MZI board; the laser-preparation breadboard
          with the lock and probe gate AOMs; the delay-stage assembly with its motorized retroreflector
          rail and PM fiber spools; and the microscope station, where the tapered-fiber positioner
          approaches the diamond sample from the left.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <CriticalityDivider label="protocol" />

      <h2>Two lasers, interleaved</h2>
      <p>
        The fiber paths drift by many optical wavelengths per second, so <em>φ</em><sub>0</sub>(<em>t</em>)
        has to be pinned in real time. It cannot be pinned with the probe itself: the probe changes
        frequency at every scan point and carries the cavity phase we are trying to measure, so a
        servo closed on it would absorb the signal. The two jobs are therefore split between two
        lasers and interleaved in time. Running them simultaneously would superimpose two fringe
        patterns on the same photodiodes; time-interleaving keeps them separate while guaranteeing
        that lock and probe sample the same optical path and the same detectors, so slow drifts cancel
        in the difference.
      </p>
      <p>One cycle has four steps:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Lock window.</strong> Only the lock laser propagates. The Red Pitaya forms
          <em>d</em> = in1 − in2, which doubles the fringe signal and cancels laser intensity noise
          common to both photodiodes, and a servo holds the interferometer at mid-fringe for 0.3 s —
          the operating point where the response to a phase perturbation is linear and steepest.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Freeze and swap.</strong> The servo output is frozen and the TTL gates swap the
          beams. Freezing is essential rather than convenient: with the lock beam off there is no
          valid error signal, and with the probe on the loop would try to servo away the cavity phase.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Probe window.</strong> The actuator executes a triangular sweep that carries the
          fringe through several periods. Sweeping deliberately, instead of reading a static level,
          makes the estimate immune to slow amplitude and offset drift — the phase is encoded in the
          <em>position</em> of the fringe pattern along the sweep, not in any absolute voltage. Each
          monotonic leg is fitted separately, and leg-to-leg disagreement is a built-in diagnostic for
          drift or actuator saturation.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Correction.</strong> The average lock-window level records where the servo actually
          parked, and each probe phase is referenced to that rather than to the nominal setpoint.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={lockProbeCycle} alt="One measured lock-probe cycle: the top panel shows the phase-control voltage servo-locked, frozen at the AOM swap, then executing a triangular sweep; the bottom panel shows both photodiodes and their difference passing through several fringe periods, with three fitted legs shaded." widths={[600, 1000, 1500]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          One measured cycle. Top: the phase-control voltage — servo-locked on the lock beam, frozen
          at the AOM swap, then sweeping (dotted lines mark the phase-modulation clamp). Bottom: both
          photodiodes and their difference; at the swap the fringe level jumps because the probe beam
          interferes at a different phase, and the shaded legs are the ones fitted.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        A complete cycle takes roughly half a second, dominated by the lock-settling window. The
        sweep's 1 ms ramp half-period is fast enough that the frozen fringe barely moves, yet slow
        enough for the AOM drive chain to follow. Ten cycles are run per frequency point and combined
        with circular statistics — phases live on a circle, so an arithmetic mean across a ±180° wrap
        would be meaningless.
      </p>

      <h2>What the lock has to fight</h2>
      <p>
        Left alone, the interferometer wanders through several full fringes in about two seconds — a
        phase drift of order 1000° per second — driven by thermal and acoustic fluctuations of the
        fiber. Engaging the servo pulls both photodiodes to the mid-fringe setpoint within a few
        milliseconds and holds them there.
      </p>
      <p>
        The loop is deliberately a <em>pure integrator</em>. The disturbances worth cancelling are
        thermal and low-frequency, where integral action gives zero steady-state error; adding
        proportional gain extends loop gain into the acoustic band and, in commissioning tests,
        increased rather than decreased the cycle-to-cycle scatter. Its gain is rescaled by the
        measured fringe slope at every calibration, so the realized bandwidth survives changes in
        fringe contrast and optical power.
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={lockAcquisition} alt="Lock acquisition: at the engagement line both photodiodes are pulled from their free-running wander to the mid-fringe setpoint within a few milliseconds, and the servo actuator settles to a quasi-static value." widths={[600, 1000, 1200]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          Lock acquisition. At the dashed line the integrator engages: both photodiodes are pulled
          from their free-running wander to the setpoint within milliseconds (insets), and the
          actuator settles to the value that cancels the instantaneous path-length drift.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The figure of merit that matters is how well a phase reproduces from cycle to cycle. Over 400
        consecutive cycles at a fixed probe frequency, the servo-referenced phase scatters with a
        <strong>circular standard deviation of 3.11°</strong>, with no visible systematic drift across
        the three-minute record. That is the single-cycle resolution of the instrument, and averaging
        <em>N</em> cycles improves the per-step uncertainty by about 1/√<em>N</em>. Four independent
        internal estimators — each photodiode alone, the difference-signal fit, and the frozen fringe
        at the swap — track each other closely, which confirms the result does not depend on which
        photodiode combination is used.
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={repeatability} alt="Repeated phase measurement over 400 cycles at a fixed probe frequency, showing all four internal estimators clustered around minus 110.7 degrees with a circular standard deviation of 3.11 degrees." widths={[600, 1000, 1240]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          400 consecutive cycles at one probe frequency, shown for all four internal estimators. The
          servo-referenced phase (red squares; band = circular mean ± circular std) reproduces to
          3.11°.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <CriticalityDivider label="arm imbalance" />

      <h2>The linear background, and why it must be small</h2>
      <p>
        The residual group-delay difference between the arms makes the measured phase ramp with laser
        frequency, advancing a full 360° every free spectral range
        <em>ν</em><sub>FSR</sub> = <em>c</em>/Δ<em>L</em><sub>opt</sub>. Wrapped into a ±180° window,
        a large enough ramp turns the data into a sawtooth in which the cavity feature is barely
        recognisable. The scale that matters is the background advance <em>per scan step</em>: at
        <em>ν</em><sub>FSR</sub> = 600 GHz a 2 GHz step advances the background by about 1° and the
        cavity feature survives wrapping intact, while at 10 GHz it advances 72°, adjacent points are
        no longer connected by less than 180°, and unwrapping becomes ambiguous. A second, subtler
        reason to balance well: an error in the assumed FSR leaves a residual slope that shrinks
        <em>quadratically</em> with growing <em>ν</em><sub>FSR</sub>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The FSR is measured with the probe far from any resonance, where the cavity phase is flat and
        the interferometer phase is a straight line in frequency. Stepping across a cavity-free 1 THz
        window, the fringe pattern translates by exactly one period per <em>ν</em><sub>FSR</sub> — so
        the FSR is literally the pitch of the diagonal stripes in the spectrogram below.
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={fsrSpectrogram} alt="Normalized fringe signal against swept phase horizontally and probe frequency vertically over a cavity-free one terahertz range, showing diagonal stripes whose pitch is the interferometer free spectral range." widths={[600, 1000, 1500]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          Fringe signal versus swept phase (horizontal) and probe frequency (vertical) across a
          cavity-free 1 THz range. The diagonal drift is the interferometer FSR: one full fringe
          period of translation per <em>ν</em><sub>FSR</sub> of laser frequency.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Fitting the per-step phases of that run gives
        <strong><em>ν</em><sub>FSR</sub> = 725.5 GHz</strong> (slope +0.496°/GHz, residual scatter
        3.4° rms — consistent with the single-cycle repeatability at the averaging used), or an
        optical path imbalance of <strong>≈ 0.41 mm</strong>. The two arms of a several-metre fiber
        interferometer are thus matched to under half a millimetre. Compensation then happens twice:
        physically, by moving the delay stage half the measured mismatch (the retroreflector is
        double-passed), and digitally, by subtracting the measured line in analysis — with any
        leftover error absorbed by a free residual-slope parameter in the cavity fit.
      </p>

      <CriticalityDivider label="automation" />

      <h2>The control application</h2>
      <p>
        Everything above — device initialization, the go/no-go optical checks, power and lock
        calibration, the scan engine, the FSR measurement and the analysis — runs in a desktop
        application that automates the measurement end to end and archives every scan with its full
        hardware configuration. Six modules sit on one dashboard, each with quick controls that run
        its main action without opening it, and a status chip summarising its state.
      </p>
      <p>
        One architectural decision is worth stating, because it is what makes the application
        trustworthy: <strong>it never re-implements the validated measurement code.</strong> The scan
        engine runs the verbatim console core in its own subprocess, while the GUI process holds a
        separate hardware session for initialization, checks and calibration. The automation is a
        wrapper around the physics code, not a rewrite of it.
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={guiDashboard} alt="The application dashboard showing six module cards - Initialization, Measure FSR, Perform Checks, Calibration, Measure Phase and Analysis - each with quick controls and a status chip, above a docked log console." widths={[600, 1000, 1300]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          The dashboard after initialization. Each card carries quick controls and a status chip;
          clicking elsewhere on a card opens the module full-screen. The log console is docked in
          every view — here showing the delay stage parked at 207.5 mm and the wavemeter connected.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Before a scan, six automated tests verify the optical paths one at a time: that each laser's
        beam responds through both arms when its gate is toggled, that the probe diode is emitting and
        its current sits in the healthy window, that the wavemeter returns a sane frequency and a
        non-flat interferogram, that the delay stage is referenced and parked, and that both the
        cavity and delay arms deliver enough power. Each step keeps its measured numbers in the row,
        so a failure is diagnosable rather than merely reported.
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={guiChecks} alt="The Perform Checks module after a run showing six passed steps, each row listing the measured gate-on and gate-off photodiode voltages and their differences, above a log of the toggles performed." widths={[600, 1000, 1300]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          Perform Checks after a real run. Every step retains the voltages it measured and the
          thresholds it applied, and the log records each output and gate toggle the step performed.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Calibration then runs in a deliberate order, because the steps depend on one another. Arm
        powers are balanced first for both lasers; the preparation-stage beams are equalized next
        under a hard cap on the instantaneous photodiode peak — measured <em>while the arms interfere</em>,
        since on a fringe maximum the fields add coherently and a cap derived from single-arm powers
        would still clip; fringe visibility is recorded for each beam as a polarization health check;
        and finally the lock calibration sweeps the actuator through a full fringe to extract the
        phase-per-volt constant, the error-slope plant gain, the mid-fringe setpoint and the actuator
        saturation limits.
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={guiCalibration} alt="The Calibration module after a run showing four passed steps and a log reporting the measured phase-per-volt constant, plant gain, fringe visibilities, setpoint offset and the resulting loop unity-gain frequency." widths={[600, 1000, 1300]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          Calibration after a real run. The log carries the numbers that matter downstream — here
          <em>K</em> = 409.5 deg/V, plant gain <em>D</em> = 4.12, visibilities 0.95 / 0.93, and the
          operating loop unity-gain frequency of about 10 Hz.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <CriticalityDivider label="results" />

      <h2>Nine cavities</h2>
      <p>
        The full protocol was applied to eleven scans covering nine distinct cavities across three
        samples, each scan comprising 146–201 frequency steps of joint phase and amplitude data, all
        processed identically. Phase and amplitude are fitted <em>together</em> with a single model
        whose free parameters include the global phase offset, a residual linear slope absorbing any
        FSR error, an amplitude normalization with a linear tilt across the scan, and an incoherent
        background fraction that floors the dip. The sign of the detuning axis and the coupling regime
        are decided by refitting under both hypotheses and keeping the lower χ².
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={cavityFits} alt="Six joint phase-amplitude cavity fits, each panel showing unwrapped FSR-compensated phase above normalized reflected amplitude, with over-coupled devices winding the phase through a full turn and under-coupled devices making a bounded excursion." widths={[600, 1000, 1283]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          Joint phase–amplitude fits for the six EG9-sample devices. Per panel: unwrapped,
          FSR-compensated phase above, normalized reflected amplitude below. Over-coupled devices wind
          the phase through a full turn; under-coupled devices make a bounded excursion that returns
          to baseline.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>The fits span the full range the theory predicts:</p>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="devices">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Cavity</th>
              <th><em>f</em><sub>res</sub> (THz)</th>
              <th><em>κ</em><sub>i</sub></th>
              <th><em>κ</em><sub>ex</sub></th>
              <th><em>κ</em> (GHz)</th>
              <th>Regime</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {devices.map((d) => (
              <tr>
                <td class="mono">{d.name}</td>
                <td class="mono num">{d.fres}</td>
                <td class="mono num">{d.ki}</td>
                <td class="mono num">{d.kex}</td>
                <td class="mono num">{d.k}</td>
                <td><span class={`regime ${d.regime.replace('-', '')}`}>{d.regime}</span></td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="tnote">
        Eight of the eleven scans; rates in GHz. The full table is in the report.
      </p>

      <p>
        The extremes are instructive. EGST3_LA_c01 is strongly over-coupled at
        <em>κ</em><sub>ex</sub>/<em>κ</em><sub>i</sub> ≈ 19; EG9_LB_b04 is clearly under-coupled at
        ≈ 0.6. The most useful case is EG9_RB_c08, which sits near critical coupling: its two
        independent measurements give <em>κ</em><sub>i</sub> ≈ <em>κ</em><sub>ex</sub> within 3 GHz and
        accordingly land on opposite sides of the regime boundary. That is exactly the ambiguity a
        magnitude-only measurement would leave <em>everywhere</em> — and which the phase measurement
        confines to the critical point itself.
      </p>

      <h2>Honest limits</h2>
      <p>
        Repeated devices reproduce <em>κ</em> at about the 15% level between sessions, and one
        resonance moved by 45 GHz between runs. Those shifts are larger than the instrument's
        single-session repeatability of a few degrees and sub-GHz, so they are attributed to the
        device and its environment — the tapered-fiber contact position, slow spectral drift of the
        structures — rather than to the interferometer. A systematic study of that drift has not been
        done.
      </p>
      <p>
        The clearest present bottleneck is the probe laser. Mode checking and re-tuning dominate the
        scan overhead near mode-hop boundaries, and a source with a larger mode-hop-free tuning range
        would remove most of the per-point tuning time and the multimode failure mode altogether.
        Independent phase control of the two arms, using a second Red Pitaya, is the other natural
        upgrade.
      </p>
      <p>
        The practical outcome is narrower than the machinery suggests, and worth stating plainly: the
        coupling regime of a fabricated device, previously ambiguous from intensity data alone, is now
        a routine screening measurement that runs without manual intervention.
      </p>

